1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,454
Two-dollar socks.

2
00:00:01,454 --> 00:00:03,899
A phone case for less than a coffee.

3
00:00:03,899 --> 00:00:08,380
Shipped from a factory in China to a front door in Ohio, for free.

4
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:13,405
That was Temu's promise when it arrived in America in September 2022.

5
00:00:13,405 --> 00:00:17,516
Less than a year and a half later, fifty-one million Americans

6
00:00:17,516 --> 00:00:21,030
were using it every month, according to Sensor Tower.

7
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:26,469
Then, in 2025, the United States shut the door that model was built on:

8
00:00:26,469 --> 00:00:32,160
the rule that let small parcels into the country without paying any customs duty.

9
00:00:32,610 --> 00:00:33,954
So here's our question.

10
00:00:33,954 --> 00:00:37,168
How did Temu sell things that cheap in the first place?

11
00:00:37,168 --> 00:00:39,330
And can it survive without that door?

12
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:44,505
The usual answers are cheap factories, burning cash, and a flood of ads.

13
00:00:44,505 --> 00:00:46,080
Each one is partly true.

14
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:48,180
None of them is the whole story.

15
00:00:51,780 --> 00:00:56,562
Temu belongs to PDD Holdings, a company officially based in Dublin,

16
00:00:56,562 --> 00:01:00,060
whose roots, and most of its money, are in China.

17
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:05,651
Its other app is Pinduoduo, one of China's biggest shopping platforms.

18
00:01:05,651 --> 00:01:09,616
It started in 2015 with what it calls "team purchase":

19
00:01:09,616 --> 00:01:14,757
if enough friends buy a product together, everyone gets a lower price.

20
00:01:14,757 --> 00:01:17,841
PDD says Pinduoduo and Temu have "the same

21
00:01:17,841 --> 00:01:20,926
value propositions and operational model".

22
00:01:20,926 --> 00:01:23,790
Temu is Pinduoduo's playbook, exported.

23
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:28,266
Temu launched in the United States in September 2022,

24
00:01:28,266 --> 00:01:33,280
reached Australia six months later, and Europe a month after that.

25
00:01:33,730 --> 00:01:37,426
PDD doesn't publish separate accounts for Temu.

26
00:01:37,426 --> 00:01:43,954
But one line of its results includes Temu's marketplace fees: transaction services.

27
00:01:43,954 --> 00:01:48,280
In 2022, it brought in about twenty-eight billion yuan.

28
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:51,583
By 2025, two hundred and fourteen billion.

29
00:01:51,583 --> 00:01:54,650
Almost eight times more in three years.

30
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:57,504
So whatever Temu did, it worked.

31
00:01:57,504 --> 00:01:58,180
Now, how?

32
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:03,174
The first answer most people give: things are made in China,

33
00:02:03,174 --> 00:02:06,160
Chinese factories are cheap, so Temu is cheap.

34
00:02:06,610 --> 00:02:09,712
But Amazon sellers buy from Chinese factories too.

35
00:02:09,712 --> 00:02:12,999
So do Walmart's suppliers, and Shein, and AliExpress.

36
00:02:12,999 --> 00:02:15,667
The difference isn't where things are made.

37
00:02:15,667 --> 00:02:18,210
It's what happens after the factory door.

38
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:23,532
A product sold in an American store usually passes through an importer,

39
00:02:23,532 --> 00:02:28,473
a distributor, a warehouse and a retailer, and each one adds its margin.

40
00:02:28,473 --> 00:02:30,257
Temu cut most of them out.

41
00:02:30,257 --> 00:02:34,580
The order went from the factory, more or less directly, to you.

42
00:02:35,030 --> 00:02:40,444
And under Temu's original model, known as "fully managed", sellers left everything,

43
00:02:40,444 --> 00:02:45,662
in Bloomberg's words, "price-setting, shipping and marketing included", to Temu.

44
00:02:45,662 --> 00:02:47,684
The factory supplied the goods.

45
00:02:47,684 --> 00:02:49,510
Temu decided what you'd pay.

46
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:53,827
That gave Temu enormous pressure on prices.

47
00:02:53,827 --> 00:03:00,123
Sellers interviewed by WIRED in 2023 said they had no say over prices.

48
00:03:00,123 --> 00:03:05,160
One put it this way: "We are working for Temu for free".

49
00:03:05,610 --> 00:03:07,471
So the factories were cheap, yes.

50
00:03:07,471 --> 00:03:11,530
But it was Temu that squeezed the margins out of everything around them.

51
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:18,230
The second answer: Temu was simply losing money on every order, to buy customers.

52
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:21,619
That's partly true, at least at the start.

53
00:03:21,619 --> 00:03:25,047
In May 2023, WIRED estimated that Temu was losing

54
00:03:25,047 --> 00:03:28,685
about thirty dollars per order in the United States.

55
00:03:28,685 --> 00:03:34,143
One brokerage figured that sending a single small parcel from Guangzhou to the

56
00:03:34,143 --> 00:03:39,320
US cost around fourteen dollars, on an average order of about twenty-five.

57
00:03:39,770 --> 00:03:41,755
That's what a land grab looks like:

58
00:03:41,755 --> 00:03:45,044
pay for the first order, and hope the customer comes back.

59
00:03:45,044 --> 00:03:48,050
But it doesn't explain how Temu kept going for years.

60
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:49,871
Look at its owner.

61
00:03:49,871 --> 00:03:53,603
PDD's profit didn't collapse while Temu expanded.

62
00:03:53,603 --> 00:03:57,412
It more than tripled, from thirty-one billion yuan

63
00:03:57,412 --> 00:04:01,220
in 2022 to one hundred and twelve billion in 2024.

64
00:04:01,670 --> 00:04:07,510
Even from April to June 2026, PDD made four billion dollars of profit in a single

65
00:04:07,510 --> 00:04:13,350
quarter, and held sixty-seven billion dollars in cash and short-term investments.

66
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:16,665
So Temu wasn't a startup betting its last dollar.

67
00:04:16,665 --> 00:04:19,003
It was a very profitable Chinese company

68
00:04:19,003 --> 00:04:21,634
choosing to spend part of its profits abroad.

69
00:04:21,634 --> 00:04:23,680
And a lot of that money bought ads.

70
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:29,506
At the Super Bowl in February 2024, Temu bought three ad slots during the game

71
00:04:29,506 --> 00:04:34,172
and two more after it, at roughly six and a half to seven million dollars for

72
00:04:34,172 --> 00:04:39,020
thirty seconds, plus more than fifteen million dollars in coupons and giveaways.

73
00:04:39,470 --> 00:04:44,256
PDD's sales and marketing spending went from fifty-four billion

74
00:04:44,256 --> 00:04:48,814
yuan in 2022 to one hundred and twenty-five billion in 2025.

75
00:04:48,814 --> 00:04:53,372
That's more than seventeen billion dollars in a single year,

76
00:04:53,372 --> 00:04:57,550
mostly, the company says, on promotion and advertising.

77
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,384
In early April 2025, Temu accounted for about a fifth of all ad impressions

78
00:05:03,384 --> 00:05:08,840
on Google Shopping in the United States, according to the ad agency Tinuiti.

79
00:05:09,290 --> 00:05:11,656
But ads only bring people to the app.

80
00:05:11,656 --> 00:05:15,686
They don't make a phone case cost two dollars on your doorstep.

81
00:05:15,686 --> 00:05:18,500
For that, the parcel itself had to be cheap.

82
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:20,930
And that's where the real answer lies.

83
00:05:22,030 --> 00:05:26,000
American customs law has a rule called "de minimis",

84
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,047
from a Latin phrase about things too small to matter.

85
00:05:30,047 --> 00:05:31,727
It dates back to 1938.

86
00:05:31,727 --> 00:05:37,606
In 2016, Congress raised its limit from two hundred dollars to eight hundred.

87
00:05:37,606 --> 00:05:42,493
Any shipment under that line, imported by one person on one day,

88
00:05:42,493 --> 00:05:47,990
came in without duties or taxes, and with only minimal data for customs.

89
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:50,451
Think about what that means.

90
00:05:50,451 --> 00:05:54,759
A store that imports ten thousand phone cases in a container

91
00:05:54,759 --> 00:05:58,709
pays duty on all of them, and files a full declaration.

92
00:05:58,709 --> 00:06:02,228
But ten thousand phone cases sent as ten thousand

93
00:06:02,228 --> 00:06:05,603
separate parcels, each to a different customer?

94
00:06:05,603 --> 00:06:06,680
No duty at all.

95
00:06:07,130 --> 00:06:09,500
That was the heart of Temu's model.

96
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:12,751
Every order left China as its own little parcel,

97
00:06:12,751 --> 00:06:15,257
mostly by air, straight to the buyer.

98
00:06:15,257 --> 00:06:18,169
In 2015, US customs handled one hundred and

99
00:06:18,169 --> 00:06:20,945
thirty-nine million de minimis shipments.

100
00:06:20,945 --> 00:06:24,535
In the 2024 fiscal year, one point three six billion.

101
00:06:24,535 --> 00:06:27,650
About three point seven million parcels a day.

102
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:31,785
A House committee estimated in 2023 that Temu and Shein

103
00:06:31,785 --> 00:06:35,537
together probably sent more than thirty percent of them.

104
00:06:35,537 --> 00:06:39,958
And e-commerce parcels filled about half of all air cargo capacity

105
00:06:39,958 --> 00:06:44,380
from China to the US, according to the freight analysts at Xeneta.

106
00:06:44,830 --> 00:06:47,367
Those light checks worried lawmakers.

107
00:06:47,367 --> 00:06:52,441
In June 2023, the House Select Committee on China said that Temu "conducts

108
00:06:52,441 --> 00:06:57,447
no audits" of its suppliers for forced labor, and concluded there was "an

109
00:06:57,447 --> 00:07:02,179
extremely high risk" that forced labor had entered its supply chains.

110
00:07:02,179 --> 00:07:05,470
The committee called these findings preliminary.

111
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:07,783
So here's the real answer.

112
00:07:07,783 --> 00:07:09,790
Not one trick, but a system.

113
00:07:09,790 --> 00:07:11,653
The factory sold directly.

114
00:07:11,653 --> 00:07:14,519
Temu set the price and handled shipping.

115
00:07:14,519 --> 00:07:19,894
Each order travelled alone, under the eight-hundred-dollar line, duty-free.

116
00:07:19,894 --> 00:07:24,480
And Pinduoduo's profits paid for the ads that filled the funnel.

117
00:07:24,930 --> 00:07:29,284
Take away one piece, and the whole thing has to change.

118
00:07:29,284 --> 00:07:32,530
In 2025, Washington took away the parcel.

119
00:07:33,630 --> 00:07:39,229
On May second, 2025, the exemption ended for goods from China and Hong Kong.

120
00:07:39,229 --> 00:07:43,650
Postal parcels were first hit with a duty of one hundred and

121
00:07:43,650 --> 00:07:48,070
twenty percent, cut to fifty-four percent twelve days later.

122
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:54,244
On August twenty-ninth, 2025, it was suspended for every country in the world.

123
00:07:54,244 --> 00:07:59,160
And Congress had already voted to repeal it in law, from July 2027.

124
00:07:59,610 --> 00:08:01,870
The door might have reopened once.

125
00:08:01,870 --> 00:08:05,859
In February 2026, the Supreme Court ruled that the emergency

126
00:08:05,859 --> 00:08:09,649
law behind many tariffs could not be used to impose them.

127
00:08:09,649 --> 00:08:14,169
But the same day, the White House put the de minimis suspension on a

128
00:08:14,169 --> 00:08:18,690
different legal footing, and customs kept it "without modification".

129
00:08:19,140 --> 00:08:23,781
In June 2026, US Customs wrote the suspension into its own regulations.

130
00:08:23,781 --> 00:08:28,684
It says it has collected more than a billion dollars of duties on more than

131
00:08:28,684 --> 00:08:33,260
two hundred and forty-six million shipments since the phase-out began.

132
00:08:33,710 --> 00:08:39,414
The tariff rates on top have changed again and again, and may keep changing.

133
00:08:39,414 --> 00:08:42,341
But the key fact for Temu hasn't moved.

134
00:08:42,341 --> 00:08:47,070
A cheap parcel from China no longer gets into America for free.

135
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:49,440
So what did Temu do?

136
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:52,046
First, it went quiet.

137
00:08:52,046 --> 00:08:56,134
In April 2025, its share of Google Shopping ads in the US

138
00:08:56,134 --> 00:09:00,150
fell from about twenty percent to zero in a single week.

139
00:09:00,150 --> 00:09:04,596
By May, Temu's daily users in the US had fallen by forty-eight

140
00:09:04,596 --> 00:09:08,540
percent compared with March, according to Sensor Tower.

141
00:09:08,990 --> 00:09:11,355
Second, it changed how it ships.

142
00:09:11,355 --> 00:09:15,050
In May 2025, Temu said that "all sales in the U.S.

143
00:09:15,050 --> 00:09:18,080
are now handled by locally based sellers,

144
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:21,554
with orders fulfilled from within the country".

145
00:09:21,554 --> 00:09:24,362
It had started down that road in 2024,

146
00:09:24,362 --> 00:09:28,870
with factories shipping goods in bulk to American warehouses.

147
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:34,792
But a container shipped to a US warehouse pays customs duty, like any other import.

148
00:09:34,792 --> 00:09:38,088
Someone has to rent the warehouse, hold the stock,

149
00:09:38,088 --> 00:09:40,659
and take the risk that it doesn't sell.

150
00:09:40,659 --> 00:09:43,692
That's exactly the cost the old model avoided.

151
00:09:43,692 --> 00:09:45,274
Prices can still be low.

152
00:09:45,274 --> 00:09:47,120
But the magic trick is gone.

153
00:09:47,570 --> 00:09:50,299
Third, it leaned on the rest of the world.

154
00:09:50,299 --> 00:09:54,392
According to analyst Ed Sander, Europe now makes up about forty

155
00:09:54,392 --> 00:09:58,290
percent of Temu's sales, and the United States about thirty.

156
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:01,271
But Europe is closing its own door.

157
00:10:01,271 --> 00:10:05,683
In 2024, four point six billion low-value parcels entered the

158
00:10:05,683 --> 00:10:10,023
European Union, more than ninety percent of them from China.

159
00:10:10,023 --> 00:10:14,434
Since July 2026, they pay a flat customs duty of three euros.

160
00:10:14,434 --> 00:10:18,340
On a five-euro item, that's a sixty percent surcharge.

161
00:10:18,790 --> 00:10:22,035
Brussels is also watching what's inside them.

162
00:10:22,035 --> 00:10:27,587
In May 2026, the European Commission fined Temu two hundred million euros for

163
00:10:27,587 --> 00:10:33,500
failing to properly assess and limit the risk of illegal products on its platform.

164
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:36,961
Temu disagrees, calls the fine disproportionate,

165
00:10:36,961 --> 00:10:40,350
and says it has since strengthened its systems.

166
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:45,580
PDD's co-chief executive, Lei Chen, has warned that cross-border orders in those

167
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:50,600
markets will face "lower fulfillment efficiency and higher costs" in the short term.

168
00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:54,852
PDD's latest results, for April to June 2026,

169
00:10:54,852 --> 00:10:59,475
show a business still growing, but more slowly, and paying for it.

170
00:10:59,475 --> 00:11:01,506
Revenue was up eight percent.

171
00:11:01,506 --> 00:11:05,009
The line that includes Temu grew thirteen percent.

172
00:11:05,009 --> 00:11:06,900
Profit fell twelve percent.

173
00:11:07,350 --> 00:11:08,660
So here's our answer.

174
00:11:08,660 --> 00:11:11,716
Yes, Temu can survive without duty-free shipping.

175
00:11:11,716 --> 00:11:15,769
It has already lived without it in America for a year and a half.

176
00:11:15,769 --> 00:11:18,950
And its parent makes billions of dollars a quarter.

177
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:21,956
But it can't survive as the same company.

178
00:11:21,956 --> 00:11:26,695
The Temu that shipped two-dollar parcels one by one from a factory in China,

179
00:11:26,695 --> 00:11:29,875
duty-free, is gone in America and fading in Europe.

180
00:11:29,875 --> 00:11:33,492
What's replacing it looks more like a regular marketplace:

181
00:11:33,492 --> 00:11:37,482
local warehouses, sellers who pay customs duty, faster delivery,

182
00:11:37,482 --> 00:11:40,600
and prices that are low, but no longer impossible.

183
00:11:41,050 --> 00:11:42,091
The strangest part?

184
00:11:42,091 --> 00:11:44,887
The old model worked so well that Amazon copied it,

185
00:11:44,887 --> 00:11:48,231
with a direct-from-China store of items under twenty dollars,

186
00:11:48,231 --> 00:11:51,410
just as the rules that made it possible were disappearing.

187
00:11:51,860 --> 00:11:56,834
So the secret of Temu was never just cheap factories, or cash, or ads.

188
00:11:56,834 --> 00:11:59,534
It was a customs rule written in 1938,

189
00:11:59,534 --> 00:12:03,086
long before anyone could order socks from a phone.

190
00:12:03,086 --> 00:12:08,060
When the rule changed, the price of a parcel changed, and so did Temu.

191
00:12:08,510 --> 00:12:13,301
If you want to see another company built on prices that look too low to be real,

192
00:12:13,301 --> 00:12:17,912
our video on Ryanair follows a fifteen-euro ticket all the way to the runway.

193
00:12:17,912 --> 00:12:19,110
Thanks for watching.
